{
  "id": "third-order-delay-edu",
  "name": "Third Order Delay",
  "description": "A higher-order delay structure using three cascaded first-order delays (pipeline delay). This creates more realistic delay behavior where output rises gradually, peaks, and approaches the input in an S-shaped pattern. Third-order delays better approximate real-world processes like shipping, processing pipelines, and biological processes where material must pass through multiple stages. Reference: Sterman, J. D. (2000). Business Dynamics: Third Order Material Delay. McGraw-Hill. Source: https://systemdynamics.org/",
